 ---
+# The infrastructure crew (crew#729 step 3): a crewAI CronJob that takes a `lane:infra` board
+# ticket to a plan comment and a pull request. No RBAC, no cluster hand; the founder merges and
+# deploys. After scheduling (its priority class), secret-store (the vault), alerts-github (the
+# App Secret its token generator reads through strict envsubst) and llm (the router it calls).
+# `wait: true` on a CronJob proves the objects applied, nothing more: a CronJob carries no
+# readiness, so kstatus reads it Current the moment it exists. Whether a run succeeds is read
+# from outside by the infra-crew drill (drills/catalogue.yaml), never from this row.
+apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
+kind: Kustomization
+metadata:
+  name: infra-crew
+  namespace: flux-system
+spec:
+  interval: 10m
+  sourceRef:
+    kind: GitRepository
+    name: flux-system
+  path: ./platform/infra-crew
+  prune: true
+  postBuild:
+    substituteFrom:
+      # OTLP_ENDPOINT and ESTATE_GITHUB_OWNER: one value each in estate-config (LAW 46).
+      - kind: ConfigMap
+        name: estate-config
+      # The estate-agents App's id and installation id for the GithubAccessToken generator;
+      # rendered into flux-system by platform/alerts-github/github-app.yaml from the vault.
+      - kind: Secret
+        name: github-app
+  dependsOn:
+    - name: scheduling
+    - name: secret-store
+    - name: alerts-github
+    - name: llm
+  wait: true
+  timeout: 10m
+---
 # crew#345: the cluster writes its own state receipt to Object Storage every 15 minutes, so no
 # session needs a kube path or `oci session authenticate` to read it (bin/idp-cluster-state).
 apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
diff --git a/docs/decisions/0014-the-home-page-speaks-plain-english.md b/docs/decisions/0014-the-home-page-speaks-plain-english.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..bc3d0b4af
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/0014-the-home-page-speaks-plain-english.md
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# The home page speaks plain English
+
+Date: 2026-09-01
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted.
+
+## Context
+
+The founder read the portal home page and could not tell what a "door" was. The
+page counted 47 of them. A word we invented forced him to ask, and an investor
+reading the same page would have to ask too. The home page also grouped every
+company, product and tool under one band, which reads as one company when there
+are two.
+
+## Decision
+
+The home page names things in plain words. A "door" is now a sign-in page, and
+the page says so. Hermes and Prospector sit under Products; mumchimp is the one
+company. Every title and description on the page is a word the founder did not
+have to ask about. A test holds the page to this: the words on the page come
+from one reviewed list, and a new invented word fails the build.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Anyone reading the home page understands it without asking. Renaming a thing
+means changing the reviewed list, which is one small change with a test on it.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/0015-the-live-feed-rides-the-state-branch.md b/docs/decisions/0015-the-live-feed-rides-the-state-branch.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..66e9bc150
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/0015-the-live-feed-rides-the-state-branch.md
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# The live feed rides the state branch
+
+Date: 2026-09-01
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted.
+
+## Context
+
+Every working session writes a short handoff to a shared feed every fifteen
+minutes. That feed lived only on one machine. The portal's live diagram is
+published from a branch that holds generated state, but the feed never reached
+it, so a person reading the portal could not see what the sessions were doing
+or hand work between machines.
+
+## Decision
+
+The published state now carries the feed. The publishing step renders the last
+two days of handoffs, removes anything secret, and writes the result next to
+the live diagram. The page that renders the catalogue picks the feed up from
+there, so the portal shows it like everything else. A test proves the rendered
+page carries the published feed.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The feed survives any one machine. A person reads the portal and sees the last
+two days of handoffs without opening a terminal. If publishing breaks, the
+test and the page go stale visibly instead of silently.
